The program is small: [38;5;10mscanf("%d", &x)[0m then a hex dump of the int's raw object representation. Two things needed care:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mx[0m stays 0 on a failed scan.** [38;5;10mscanf[0m leaves the variable untouched on matching failure or EOF, so [38;5;10m""[0m, [38;5;10m"abc"[0m, and a lone [38;5;10m"-"[0m all print [38;5;10m00000000[0m.[0m[0m
- **glibc's [38;5;10m%d[0m overflow quirk.** glibc converts the digits with [38;5;10mstrtol[0m (64-bit [38;5;10mlong[0m here), which saturates at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m, then truncates to [38;5;10mint[0m. So [38;5;10m99999999999999999999999999[0m → [38;5;10mffffffff[0m (-1) while [38;5;10m4294967296[0m → [38;5;10m00000000[0m. I reproduced this saturate-then-truncate path rather than treating it as a bug; a naive [38;5;10mstr::parse::<i32>()[0m would have gotten these wrong.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Byte order uses [38;5;10mto_ne_bytes[0m to mirror the [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m of the raw representation. Implementation is safe Rust throughout — a small buffered byte reader with single-byte pushback stands in for the [38;5;10mgetc[0m/[38;5;10mungetc[0m pair scanf uses, so no more input is consumed than the C would consume.[0m[0m
